encodes income.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest privacy-respecting approach that still follows \"only income and debts\": redact every figure derived from income or debt: 4,800, 350, 1,950, 2,850, 3.08, 18,400? Wait 18,400 = 12,400 + 6,000 — that's fine, doesn't involve income. Hmm, but \"projected balance after reaching the goal\" is just 12,400+6,000. Fine to keep.\n\nBut hold on — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look at what's actually in the files first, then decide. Let me find the session logs.\n\nAlso, there may be multiple files: the current session's .jsonl, plus possibly a summary/compaction state. There might also be `~/.claude/history` or shell-snapshots etc. Let me search broadly under ~/.claude for the figures.\n\nPlan:\n1. Find files containing \"4,800\" or \"4800\" or \"debt\" or \"1,950\" under ~/.claude (and /workspace? budget.md is user's own file — they said keep... well they said session logs; budget.md is not a log; leave it).\n2. Carefully redact within JSONL string values.\n\nOne risk: the current session file is being appended to live by the harness as I work. Editing it while the session is active — new lines get appended, that's fine; my edits to earlier lines persist. But also my current turn (this redaction work) will itself be logged, including... hmm, my tool calls that read the file and grep output showing the numbers would themselves introduce new mentions of the numbers into the log! That's a real concern: to redact, I'll run commands whose outputs contain the numbers, and those outputs get logged, re-introducing the data.\n\nTo avoid re-introducing: I should avoid printing the actual values into the transcript. Use grep -c / -l to find files, then run a script that does replacements without echoing matched content, and print only counts of replacements. And in my final summary, don't repeat the figures (the user's own message earlier in the log also contains them... wait — the user's original message \"My monthly take-home income is $